rger as `Rounds`, so a conflict starts a conflict round. In it the controller fetches `main` itself, trial-merges it into a scratch worktree (no commit, attributes from the empty tree, no rerere, renormalize or `-X`), and gives the worker an untrusted `conflict.md` (capped, escaped paths; no hunks). The first agent step starts with that merge in its worktree. After the step the controller runs no git in the agent's worktree: it reads the recorded conflicted files for leftover markers (advisory), measures the PR's own diff against the fetched `main` tip, not the job's base, and hands the committer the full diff against `main` with `MergeParent` set to that tip. The code lane does not run `git merge`: it fetches `main` into a private ref and refuses unless it equals `MergeParent` (`ErrMergeParent`), requires the branch still at the job's base (`ErrConflict`, no retry), writes the declared files over main's tree, and checks that the result differs from `main` only in declared paths that pass `CheckCodePath`, so `.github/`, `.ard/`, `.gitmodules` and `.gitattributes` equal `main` byte for byte. It commits with parents `[branch tip, main tip]` and pushes with a lease on the branch tip (a merge whose tree equals main's is still committed). A GitHub rejection of a merge that brings in workflow changes ends `needs-human`. The loop counts a conflict round as landed only when the head is a merge that includes the round's head and whose second parent is on `main`. The conflict-round section of the worker prompt is a separate human PR.\n \n **The merger takes it from here.** A worker PR stays a draft until every requested review passes at its current head; then the controller marks it ready for review. The merger merges ready PRs that also pass its gates.\n \n@@ -414,7 +414,7 @@ Only ready (non-draft) PRs the App opened on `worker/<role>-<issue#>-<slug>` wit\n - every requested `ard:review:<role>` is `:done` and none is `:changes` or `:running` (any `ard:review:*:changes` or `: